[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186068695,
      "id": "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c",
      "title": "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/109fbe887eeca5ab5f7cc5bd8dfbc01d87105fdf6a04663757493c94bed0d55b.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-4$\n\n- **II.** $x=2$\n\n- **III.** $x=6$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $-4.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$ Since there is a jump at $x=2,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of  $x=6$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and III only\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186068695/plots/d2cae49f15c5f46f6c6fb29b0b6e79b7b0b8cf4aa1145a4911620c05f7b1ec1b.png)",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "false",
